1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,296
A maths test is marked out of one hundred.

2
00:00:03,296 --> 00:00:06,289
Any mark from zero to
one hundred is allowed.

3
00:00:06,689 --> 00:00:10,832
But when the results go up, the
lowest mark in the class is forty one,

4
00:00:10,832 --> 00:00:12,712
and the highest is ninety six.

5
00:00:13,162 --> 00:00:16,357
Aarav noticed this on the
college notice board today.

6
00:00:16,707 --> 00:00:19,410
So which is the real answer for the marks?

7
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:22,543
Zero to one hundred, or
forty one to ninety six?

8
00:00:23,043 --> 00:00:24,154
Both are right.

9
00:00:24,154 --> 00:00:27,636
Maths gives them two names:
the codomain and the range.

10
00:00:27,636 --> 00:00:30,055
Today we meet both, with the domain.

11
00:00:30,705 --> 00:00:31,757
Quick recall.

12
00:00:31,757 --> 00:00:36,545
A function sends every element of one
set to exactly one element of another

13
00:00:36,545 --> 00:00:37,445
set.

14
00:00:37,421 --> 00:00:39,180
We write f, from A to B.

15
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:41,207
A is where the inputs come from.

16
00:00:41,207 --> 00:00:43,002
B is where the outputs land.

17
00:00:43,402 --> 00:00:45,151
The domain is the set A.

18
00:00:45,151 --> 00:00:47,730
It holds every input the function takes.

19
00:00:48,130 --> 00:00:50,002
The codomain is the set B.

20
00:00:50,002 --> 00:00:53,480
It holds every output the
function is allowed to give.

21
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,908
The range is the set of outputs
the function actually gives.

22
00:00:57,908 --> 00:01:00,123
These outputs are called images.

23
00:01:00,773 --> 00:01:01,885
Watch one run.

24
00:01:01,885 --> 00:01:04,111
A is one, two, three, four.

25
00:01:04,111 --> 00:01:06,553
B is the numbers from one to eight.

26
00:01:06,553 --> 00:01:08,906
The rule is f of x equals two x.

27
00:01:09,306 --> 00:01:10,632
One goes to two.

28
00:01:10,632 --> 00:01:12,008
Two goes to four.

29
00:01:12,008 --> 00:01:13,437
Three goes to six.

30
00:01:13,437 --> 00:01:14,916
Four goes to eight.

31
00:01:15,316 --> 00:01:17,656
Every input got exactly one arrow.

32
00:01:17,656 --> 00:01:20,016
So the whole of A is the domain.

33
00:01:20,416 --> 00:01:21,502
Now look at B.

34
00:01:21,502 --> 00:01:23,800
Two, four, six and eight are hit.

35
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,519
One, three, five and seven are never hit.

36
00:01:26,919 --> 00:01:29,446
So the range is two, four, six, eight.

37
00:01:29,446 --> 00:01:32,240
It sits inside the
codomain, but it is smaller.

38
00:01:32,891 --> 00:01:35,400
Two words you will meet in every exam.

39
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:36,784
f of three is six.

40
00:01:36,784 --> 00:01:40,926
So six is the image of three,
and three is the preimage of six.

41
00:01:41,676 --> 00:01:44,767
Every element of the domain
has exactly one image.

42
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:49,173
But an element of the codomain can
have no preimage at all, like five.

43
00:01:49,823 --> 00:01:50,899
Side by side.

44
00:01:50,899 --> 00:01:55,400
The codomain is chosen when we write
the function: the allowed outputs,

45
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,861
like marks from zero to one hundred.

46
00:01:58,261 --> 00:02:02,668
The range is found by running the
function: the outputs that really appear,

47
00:02:02,668 --> 00:02:04,567
like forty one to ninety six.

48
00:02:04,967 --> 00:02:08,063
So the range is always a
subset of the codomain.

49
00:02:08,063 --> 00:02:11,550
They can be equal, but the
range can never be bigger.

50
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:13,216
Now you try.

51
00:02:13,216 --> 00:02:16,664
f goes from N to N, and
f of x equals two x.

52
00:02:16,664 --> 00:02:18,807
Is the range the whole of N?

53
00:02:18,807 --> 00:02:20,133
Pause and decide.

54
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:24,233
No!

55
00:02:23,793 --> 00:02:26,989
The outputs are two, four, six, and so on.

56
00:02:26,989 --> 00:02:31,839
Odd numbers are never hit, so the
range is only the even natural numbers.

57
00:02:32,489 --> 00:02:33,572
Remember this.

58
00:02:33,572 --> 00:02:35,809
The codomain is what is allowed.

59
00:02:35,809 --> 00:02:38,198
The range is what actually happens.

60
00:02:38,898 --> 00:02:41,196
Now let us see this on a graph.

61
00:02:41,196 --> 00:02:45,115
Take f of x equals x squared,
for x from minus a to a.

62
00:02:45,515 --> 00:02:48,682
The domain is a stretch
of the x axis, in blue.

63
00:02:48,682 --> 00:02:51,849
The range is a stretch
of the y axis, in coral.

64
00:02:52,249 --> 00:02:54,389
Watch a grow from one to three.

65
00:02:54,389 --> 00:02:57,060
The domain widens, and the range climbs.

66
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:00,540
Zero to one, then zero to
four, then zero to nine.

67
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:05,428
To read a range from any graph, slide
every point of the curve across to

68
00:03:05,428 --> 00:03:06,374
the y axis.

69
00:03:06,374 --> 00:03:08,661
The shadow it leaves is the range.

70
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:10,211
Quick one.

71
00:03:10,185 --> 00:03:14,918
If x can be any real number, is
minus one in the range of x squared?

72
00:03:14,918 --> 00:03:16,154
Pause and think.

73
00:03:19,354 --> 00:03:20,254
No!

74
00:03:19,832 --> 00:03:21,805
A square is never negative.

75
00:03:21,805 --> 00:03:26,736
The curve never goes below the x
axis, so the range is zero and above.

76
00:03:27,386 --> 00:03:29,122
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:29,472 --> 00:03:32,897
A is minus two, minus one, zero, one, two.

78
00:03:32,897 --> 00:03:37,166
f goes from A to Z, and f of
x equals x squared plus one.

79
00:03:37,166 --> 00:03:39,779
Find the domain, codomain and range.

80
00:03:40,279 --> 00:03:41,179
Step one.

81
00:03:41,092 --> 00:03:45,685
The domain is A itself: minus
two, minus one, zero, one and two.

82
00:03:46,435 --> 00:03:47,335
Step two.

83
00:03:47,297 --> 00:03:50,494
The codomain is Z, the
set of all integers.

84
00:03:51,244 --> 00:03:52,144
Step three.

85
00:03:52,141 --> 00:03:53,389
Find every image.

86
00:03:53,389 --> 00:03:55,583
Five, two, one, two, five.

87
00:03:56,333 --> 00:03:57,233
Step four.

88
00:03:57,229 --> 00:03:58,867
Write each image once.

89
00:03:58,867 --> 00:04:01,256
The range is one, two and five.

90
00:04:02,006 --> 00:04:05,580
Notice that two has two
preimages, minus one and one.

91
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:06,801
That is allowed.

92
00:04:06,801 --> 00:04:09,044
Many inputs may share one output.

93
00:04:09,694 --> 00:04:11,160
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:11,510 --> 00:04:15,139
Find the range of x squared,
for x from minus two to one.

95
00:04:15,139 --> 00:04:17,583
Many students put in only the two ends.

96
00:04:17,583 --> 00:04:20,501
They get one and four,
and write one to four.

97
00:04:20,901 --> 00:04:24,938
But zero lies inside the domain,
and zero squared is zero.

98
00:04:24,938 --> 00:04:27,385
The curve dips to zero in between.

99
00:04:27,385 --> 00:04:29,832
The correct range is zero to four.

100
00:04:30,482 --> 00:04:31,964
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:31,964 --> 00:04:36,726
In JEE Main, as find the range, or
the codomain that equals the range.

102
00:04:36,726 --> 00:04:40,057
And in PUC boards, as
find the domain and range.

103
00:04:40,657 --> 00:04:41,826
Exam level now.

104
00:04:41,826 --> 00:04:44,224
What is the range of this function?

105
00:04:44,224 --> 00:04:45,443
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:50,843 --> 00:04:52,202
The answer is B.

107
00:04:52,202 --> 00:04:53,499
Three to twelve.

108
00:04:53,999 --> 00:04:55,382
Complete the square.

109
00:04:55,382 --> 00:05:00,362
x squared minus four x plus seven is x
minus two, whole squared, plus three.

110
00:05:00,862 --> 00:05:05,596
x runs from minus one to four, so x
minus two runs from minus three to

111
00:05:05,596 --> 00:05:06,496
two.

112
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:09,042
Its square runs from zero to nine.

113
00:05:09,042 --> 00:05:11,831
Zero, because x minus two can be zero.

114
00:05:11,831 --> 00:05:13,609
Nine, from minus three.

115
00:05:14,109 --> 00:05:15,009
Add three.

116
00:05:14,943 --> 00:05:16,918
The range is three to twelve.

117
00:05:16,918 --> 00:05:20,915
The two ends alone give seven
to twelve: option A, the trap.

118
00:05:21,515 --> 00:05:22,974
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:22,974 --> 00:05:24,474
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:27,674 --> 00:05:30,672
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:31,072 --> 00:05:31,972
One.

122
00:05:31,547 --> 00:05:35,153
f of x equals x squared,
on the set one, two, three.

123
00:05:35,153 --> 00:05:36,449
What is the range?

124
00:05:37,349 --> 00:05:38,249
Two.

125
00:05:37,821 --> 00:05:40,731
Can the range ever be
bigger than the codomain?

126
00:05:41,631 --> 00:05:42,531
Three.

127
00:05:42,221 --> 00:05:46,164
Find the range of x squared,
for x from minus three to two.

128
00:05:47,264 --> 00:05:51,729
Next time: Diya's body temperature
gives one number for every hour of the

129
00:05:51,729 --> 00:05:52,629
day.

130
00:05:52,231 --> 00:05:54,863
Functions like that have a special name.

131
00:05:54,863 --> 00:05:56,370
Real valued functions.
